# what is in the first half

**The look is Darwinia, scaled down, in dots.** Coloured dots rather than sprites
or models, a much smaller field than the reference, and **shadows** — which is
the detail that says the dots are objects standing on ground rather than marks on
a surface. It is the first thing in this project that says what a soldier looks
like, and it says: not much, on purpose, at a scale where a thousand of them read
as weather.

Note what it does *not* contradict. Vision 2 has a 3D RTS camera and a tower with
a model and runes projected onto the world. Dots and shadows fit inside that —
the stone is detailed because you are meant to touch it; the bodies are dots
because there are a thousand of them and you are meant to read the shape they
make.

**The enemy is not undead. It is a statue.** Earth-wizarded, skeleton-shaped,
made of **coal**. That is a much better thing than a skeleton, because it says
somebody *made* these — there is a wizard, or there was one — and it fits the
setting the documents already have, where an automated war keeps running and
nobody remembers who started it. Coal also burns, which is not nothing.

**It emits fear, and fear is the enemy's actual weapon.** Not damage. The statue
is "definitely slayable tho, just gotta be a stronger spirit," which makes the
fight a contest of nerve rather than of hit points.

**Fear does three things, and the third is the interesting one:**

- paralysing
- demoralising
- *"subtlely diminishing decisionmaking"*

The third is a status effect on a **player**, not on a body. Nothing else in this
game reaches through the screen like that, and it is either the best idea here or
the one to be most careful with.

**"fear is evil. it is inflicted."** Two sentences, and they are a design rule:
fear is not an environmental hazard or a resource, it is something one thing does
to another on purpose. It has an author.

**Sunlight paladins stand against it** — water, magic, and boots on. Sunlight
against coal, water against earth. The counter structure is elemental and it is
already legible before anybody writes a stat.

**And then the lawyers.** Kept exactly as written because it is not a mechanic
and should not be turned into one:

> the only people who want to change anything are lawyers. everyone else are
> their employers. they fight for a cause, and everyone's happy because it feels
> like you're making a difference in the world. And maybe you are. But you better
> be overcoming the fight, at a measurable pace,

It ends on a comma. That is left alone.

What it is *about*, as far as this project is concerned: **the measurable pace.**
This is a game where the frontline must move, where push depth is a small integer
somebody can read, and where the whole premise is that a war which does not
advance is a war nobody is really fighting. "You better be overcoming the fight,
at a measurable pace" is the design pillar written as an accusation.

# what is in the second half

An economy, and it is a large one. Handled as questions rather than as answers,
in the open-questions page under **F30**, because it rewrites the second economy
from the ground up:

- **Resource stops being one number.** There are as many kinds as there are
  attribute scores, each with a colour.
- **Colour is not the only channel.** Each kind also has its own *display type* —
  a bar, a flowy circle script, pips one to six, a playing card with a randomly
  generated suit. Written down as an accessibility requirement and worth reading
  as a design principle: **never encode meaning in hue alone.**
- **You farm what the enemy fields.** Killing a wave unit carrying a colour pays
  that colour. A captain pays three.
- **A commander's roster decides the shape of its bounty** — "3 blue die for
  every 1 green die and every 5 red die" — which means **the enemy's commander
  selection determines what you can afford**, and that is a loop this design did
  not previously have.
- **Some commanders just field the kingdom troops**, who are guys in historical
  period armour, FMV animated. Not everything is wizardry.
- **Buying a hero is a set of bids rather than a price.** Roll a die per
  attribute; picking high costs more of that colour, picking low costs less.
- **All heroes are roughly equivalent and counter each other**, in the manner of
  Warcraft Rumble — and the instruction is explicit that what is being copied is
  **the relationship graph, not the numbers.**
